1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Business jets that travel five times the speed of sound.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
And an at-home jet hanger next to your garage.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Plush airborne office suites.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
And cabin walls lined with imported silk.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Homemade jets for under a million dollars.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
And advice on buying previously owned jets from a Klingon.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, Private Jets on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
When it comes to jets, there are always those who are driven to leave the beaten path.

9
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:53,640
Who seek to own the unique, the unusual, the exclusive one and only.

10
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:57,520
One way to accomplish this is with a jet conversion.

11
00:00:57,521 --> 00:01:06,520
For example, you can buy a new private jet and have it flown to a conversion center where they will install a custom interior.

12
00:01:06,521 --> 00:01:13,520
Or, you can pick up a previously owned jet and let a conversion center upgrade the avionics and the interior.

13
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:20,520
You can even buy used military jets that are already converted to meet current FAA standards.

14
00:01:20,521 --> 00:01:23,520
That's what Michael Dorn did.

15
00:01:23,521 --> 00:01:32,680
If you don't recognize him without his makeup, he's the Klingon Lieutenant Warp from the popular television and film series Star Trek The Next Generation.

16
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:43,680
Michael's also been an armchair expert on aviation for 30 years, a pilot for almost 15, and has been flying jets since 1992.

17
00:01:43,681 --> 00:01:44,381
1992.

18
00:01:44,382 --> 00:01:53,120
I've gone from regular airplanes, Cessnas, single engines, to twins, to jets, to military

19
00:01:53,121 --> 00:01:56,362
jets, ex-military jets. I had a T-33, an F-86.

20
00:01:56,363 --> 00:01:59,040
My passion is military jets right now.

21
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:06,880
The first professionally converted military planes were World War II bombers and transports

22
00:02:06,881 --> 00:02:09,620
that were made into corporate carriers for VIPs.

23
00:02:09,621 --> 00:02:16,620
The first large jet converted for private use was a 707 in 1961.

24
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:19,620
It became known as Air Force One.

25
00:02:19,621 --> 00:02:26,620
In the 50s, North American came up with the idea of taking the Sabre jet,

26
00:02:26,621 --> 00:02:30,620
which was a very popular and very strong design,

27
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:37,620
and make a passenger jet for the military, for the Air Force.

28
00:02:37,621 --> 00:02:45,160
And if you look at this airplane and the F-86, you'll see same designs in terms of the wing and the landing gear.

29
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:49,040
So they took all the best things about the Sabrejet and incorporated this airplane.

30
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:56,340
The Sabreliner also offers a very roomy cockpit, as well as remarkable speed and range.

31
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:02,280
It does .82 Mach, which is .82 percentage speed of sound, all day long.

32
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:06,520
It has a high ceiling. It can go up to 43,000, 45,000.

33
00:03:06,521 --> 00:03:09,760
and also the range is about 1,400 miles.

34
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:14,520
So it's one stop cross-country either way, no matter what winds you have.

35
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:17,580
Although the Learjet, which is the other airplane I was looking at,

36
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:19,720
has this blazing speed off the ground,

37
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:24,820
once this airplane gets going, it is just as fast.

38
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:29,560
If you're considering the purchase of a previously owned jet,

39
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:31,100
Michael has some advice.

40
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:34,580
When you are thinking about an aircraft like this,

41
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:35,940
you can either do one of two things.

42
00:03:35,941 --> 00:03:41,980
You either go to a broker that you know, or you can go and trade a plane, which is a periodical,

43
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:49,680
or you can go into aircraft.com on the Internet or the Controller, which is another magazine that's big for these airplanes.

44
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:52,680
Next, decide on your mission.

45
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:54,460
Where you intend to fly.

46
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:58,860
Will you be traveling under 800 miles or more than 1,000 per trip?

47
00:03:58,861 --> 00:04:05,740
and once you decide on the aircraft, call up the guy, start haggling.

48
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:07,360
That's what you have a broker for.

49
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:12,580
And if you're smart, you will have a pre-buy inspection

50
00:04:12,581 --> 00:04:15,940
where they take the airplane, tear it apart, inspect it,

51
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:17,480
make sure everything's there, everything works.

52
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:20,620
And then you go back to the person.

53
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:23,820
You say, okay, I want it, or let's talk some more.

54
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:25,080
And that's how you do it.

55
00:04:25,081 --> 00:04:32,080
The inspection is something you pay for, but it's worth it to know what condition your jet is in.

56
00:04:32,081 --> 00:04:41,080
Michael's Sabre Liner was already upgraded with air conditioning that works on the ground, autopilot, and a global positioning system.

57
00:04:41,081 --> 00:04:51,080
According to Michael, a vintage jet aircraft like this Sabre Liner could sell for between $350,000 and $800,000.

58
00:04:51,081 --> 00:04:56,160
The best part about these airplanes is the convenience that it gives you.

59
00:04:56,161 --> 00:04:58,700
You don't have to deal with the baggage carousel.

60
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:01,160
You don't have to deal with going through the terminal.

61
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:04,220
Plus, you can get closer to your destination.

62
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:07,380
You don't have to fly into the big airports.

63
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:10,820
You can fly into the little ones outlying, the kind of little satellite airports.

64
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:17,640
If you are not someone with a passion for flying the older converted military and commercial jets,

65
00:05:17,641 --> 00:05:24,320
If you want something brand new, a conversion that's luxurious with state-of-the-art avionics,

66
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:31,160
and you're willing to spend a lot of money, consider the Associated Air Center in Dallas, Texas,

67
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:36,240
a company that creates very high-end, lavish jet interiors for their customers.

68
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:41,920
We rely for the most part on the customer bringing to us an aircraft.

69
00:05:41,921 --> 00:05:46,620
and you can buy it brand new from Boeing or Airbus

70
00:05:46,621 --> 00:05:49,940
or you can buy it from a leasing company.

71
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:54,560
Once the new owner has made the initial jet purchase,

72
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,720
the aircraft has flown to the Associated Air Center at Love Field in Dallas.

73
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:03,740
We have done what we call the nose-to-tail conversions

74
00:06:03,741 --> 00:06:08,040
in which we take a regular airliner,

75
00:06:08,041 --> 00:06:14,960
which already comes with lavatories and galleys and sidewalls and bins and so forth.

76
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:18,160
And then we upgraded.

77
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,520
We converted into a VIP.

78
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:34,720
This is where you will give your Boeing BBJ or Airbus ACJ the royal treatment.

79
00:06:34,721 --> 00:06:39,200
as the Associated Air Center guides you through the decision process.

80
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:45,180
First, you get a reading from the customer.

81
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:48,200
What do you want your aircraft for?

82
00:06:48,820 --> 00:06:51,140
Is long-range, short-range?

83
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:56,580
Do you want a bedroom, or do you rather have a lounge or a meeting area, a dining area?

84
00:06:56,581 --> 00:07:02,060
Then we try to get an idea of the budget that the customer has

85
00:07:02,061 --> 00:07:09,180
in order to start picking fabrics and stones and veneers and finishes

86
00:07:09,181 --> 00:07:11,600
and plating of the metal parts.

87
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:16,440
Another area that is very important to our customers is the entertainment.

88
00:07:16,441 --> 00:07:20,760
So we sit down and try to define how many sources,

89
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:25,300
how many viewing areas, how many monitors, if any.

90
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:31,520
Ron Tudor, president of the Tudor Saliba Corporation,

91
00:07:31,521 --> 00:07:37,520
an international construction company, wanted an elegantly outfitted Boeing BBJ to use for

92
00:07:37,521 --> 00:07:43,874
both his family and his business. He sent his jet to the Associated Air Center.

93
00:07:43,875 --> 00:07:44,920
The engineers

94
00:07:44,921 --> 00:07:54,000
and designers turned the BBJ's empty interior into a luxurious airborne home for a family

95
00:07:54,001 --> 00:07:58,900
of four that could also double as an airborne office, conference room and hotel suite.

96
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:10,520
The main lounge is designed for 12 people with all kinds of great toys.

97
00:08:10,521 --> 00:08:19,520
The aircraft is equipped with four video monitors, two 42-inch plasma screen monitors,

98
00:08:19,521 --> 00:08:23,520
one in the main cabin, one in the master stateroom.

99
00:08:23,521 --> 00:08:28,520
Videos via DVD or VHS are available.

100
00:08:28,521 --> 00:08:33,280
You can watch two separate videos at any time.

101
00:08:33,281 --> 00:08:42,940
We have a sophisticated audio system where you can play multiple CDs with headphones at different listening stations or speakers throughout the cabin.

102
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:56,500
The galley is a state-of-the-art gourmet kitchen with a gas range, microwave, dishwasher, convection oven, and cappuccino maker.

103
00:08:56,501 --> 00:09:01,260
and a flight attendant who also happens to be a first-rate chef.

104
00:09:02,780 --> 00:09:07,560
Between the main lounge and galley is a marble wet bar that's fully stocked.

105
00:09:08,700 --> 00:09:11,880
Then right behind it has a guest lavatory

106
00:09:11,881 --> 00:09:16,120
and what we call a kid's room for the principal's children.

107
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:24,760
And then the master suite has a big bedroom.

108
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:33,500
I'm in a big lavatory with a shower.

109
00:09:33,501 --> 00:09:39,740
So it's set up more like a home, away from home.

110
00:09:39,741 --> 00:09:46,800
In my nine years with the company and 30 years in the business, I don't recall ever doing

111
00:09:46,801 --> 00:09:47,800
two airplanes alike.

112
00:09:47,801 --> 00:09:55,440
And basically that's what people are looking for, the exclusive, the personal touch.

113
00:09:55,441 --> 00:10:01,440
The cost of this type of conversion will run between 12 and 15 million dollars.

114
00:10:01,441 --> 00:10:07,060
But if you don't happen to have the price of a new BBJ and another 15 million for the

115
00:10:07,061 --> 00:10:12,320
custom interior, consider a do-it-yourself jet.

116
00:10:12,321 --> 00:10:17,160
At Maverick Air in Fremont, Colorado, they're hard at work creating a twin jet aircraft

117
00:10:17,161 --> 00:10:23,240
you can build from a kit with an estimated range of about a thousand miles.

118
00:10:23,241 --> 00:10:36,840
It will cruise at a maximum speed of 440 miles per hour.

119
00:10:36,841 --> 00:10:43,980
With a service ceiling of 30,000 feet, this kit also includes a pressurized cabin.

120
00:10:43,981 --> 00:10:49,880
If you're looking to build your own jet, plan on spending in the neighborhood of $650,000,

121
00:10:49,881 --> 00:10:53,760
depending on who assembles it.

122
00:10:53,761 --> 00:10:58,300
Previously owned and kit-built jets open the jet ownership market to a broader range of

123
00:10:58,301 --> 00:11:00,920
consumers.

124
00:11:00,921 --> 00:11:05,780
But when it comes to spending money in the tens of millions of dollars, expensive interior

125
00:11:05,781 --> 00:11:09,880
jet conversions are just the beginning.

126
00:11:09,881 --> 00:11:16,880
Next, high-end Megabucks transatlantic private jets, flying offices for the Fortune 500.

127
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:26,880
When it comes to high-end business jet travelers, there is a new must-have.

128
00:11:26,881 --> 00:11:30,880
Private jets the size of giant commercial airliners.

129
00:11:30,881 --> 00:11:37,880
These office suites in the sky are capable of traveling across whole continents and oceans, without refueling.

130
00:11:37,881 --> 00:11:44,880
The most popular are built by Boeing, Bombardier, Gulfstream and Airbus.

131
00:11:44,881 --> 00:11:50,780
Some of these companies won't even discuss price, unless you're a serious buyer.

132
00:11:51,580 --> 00:11:56,300
But figure these aircraft will cost anywhere between $35 to $50 million.

133
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:01,620
So, who buys these lavish airborne corporate suites?

134
00:12:02,260 --> 00:12:03,680
And are they worth it?

135
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:07,920
I think there's a myth about who really owns these aircraft.

136
00:12:07,921 --> 00:12:15,280
First of all, most celebrities, unless the very select few, don't own these aircraft.

137
00:12:15,740 --> 00:12:16,440
They use them.

138
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:23,360
They're either provided by agents or owners or studios to get people around.

139
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:29,300
The real owners and operators of these aircraft are people that run large, mid-sized corporations

140
00:12:29,301 --> 00:12:32,360
who have many business interests that need to get around.

141
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:36,600
Heads of state are also among the clientele.

142
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:44,480
In the late 1990s, Kuwait Airways bought three Gulfstream Fives for that country's senior government officials.

143
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:54,680
A Gulfstream III, or C-20, was assigned to General Norman Schwarzkopf at the beginning of Desert Shield, which became Desert Storm.

144
00:12:55,440 --> 00:13:00,920
It was given to him on a 24-hour-a-day, 7-day-a-week basis.

145
00:13:00,921 --> 00:13:11,900
It became his flying office, his command center, and the best way for him to communicate with the military leaders and advisors of all of the Mideastern countries.

146
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:18,600
The concept of this flying corporate office obviously appeals to some people.

147
00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:21,880
It's phenomenal how big it is, how roomy it is.

148
00:13:22,320 --> 00:13:25,460
You can have conferences going on in one conference room.

149
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:26,900
You could set up another conference room.

150
00:13:26,901 --> 00:13:30,580
You could have secretarial staff in there taking care of things.

151
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:35,440
your data links to corporate headquarters to make sure you're connected.

152
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:37,640
You can take the show on the road.

153
00:13:44,220 --> 00:13:46,900
That road might be an airborne highway

154
00:13:46,901 --> 00:13:50,420
that takes a top management team from Los Angeles

155
00:13:50,421 --> 00:13:54,260
all the way to Paris, London, or Rome,

156
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:57,480
or from New York to Tokyo, nonstop.

157
00:13:57,481 --> 00:14:04,560
The first of these super long-range business aircraft to hit the market was the Gulfstream

158
00:14:04,561 --> 00:14:08,320
5 in 1997.

159
00:14:08,321 --> 00:14:12,860
The Seagram company was its first customer.

160
00:14:12,861 --> 00:14:21,140
The G5 was followed by Bombardier's Global Express, then the Boeing Business Jet.

161
00:14:21,141 --> 00:14:27,620
the European manufactured Airbus A319 corporate jetliner became available in late 1997.

162
00:14:29,380 --> 00:14:34,740
The Airbus corporate jet is comparable in size, function and price to the BBJ.

163
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:39,900
Although with 6,000 cubic feet of living space, it is a bit larger.

164
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:48,660
But there are other differences, like its Category 3B all-weather landing system,

165
00:14:48,661 --> 00:14:50,960
used for low-visibility landings.

166
00:14:51,140 --> 00:14:58,840
50-meter runway visual range, an important operational capability that's not found on many commercial airliners or corporate jets.

167
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:05,160
The Boeing Business Jet, or BBJ, is a deluxe version of its 737 commercial jet,

168
00:15:06,160 --> 00:15:08,980
designed especially for corporate and VIP clients.

169
00:15:11,220 --> 00:15:17,100
Inside the cabin, there are 807 square feet of floor space, larger than some apartments.

170
00:15:17,101 --> 00:15:23,660
To expand the range on the Boeing business jet, they've added winglets.

171
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:30,160
This increases the airflow over the wing, which steps up the efficiency by approximately 5%,

172
00:15:30,161 --> 00:15:35,540
making it possible for the BBJ to land in less than 6,000 feet of runway.

173
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:37,200
But that's not all.

174
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:41,700
It's equipped with TCAS, which is Traffic Collision Avoidance System.

175
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:46,620
It lets us see other aircraft in our vicinity to avoid them.

176
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:50,900
It is also equipped with wind shear avoidance system.

177
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:55,160
In weather conditions, it might be dangerous to the aircraft.

178
00:15:55,440 --> 00:15:57,600
It alerts us prior to us entering those.

179
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:07,540
The addition of auxiliary fuel tanks provides owners with a business jet that has a maximum range capability of 7,100 miles.

180
00:16:09,420 --> 00:16:16,700
Bombardier, the company that bought Learjet back in 1992, also builds another business jet, the Global Express.

181
00:16:17,100 --> 00:16:25,160
It has an overall length of 99 feet and a wingspan of 94 feet, and is the only aircraft

182
00:16:25,161 --> 00:16:29,040
in this class that's built from scratch.

183
00:16:29,041 --> 00:16:33,000
The others are modified versions of aircraft that already exist.

184
00:16:33,001 --> 00:16:40,340
The Global Express is really the pinnacle of aviation technology.

185
00:16:40,341 --> 00:16:43,660
It's got triple redundancy in all of its systems.

186
00:16:43,661 --> 00:16:49,020
It's been designed because of its long range to be able to fly over not only Atlantic but the Pacific Ocean.

187
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:58,020
So it's got to be able to conform to all of the requirements for extended range operation of a two-engine aircraft over long expanses of water.

188
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:07,700
Triple redundancy is a safety feature that means in the event of a failure of any system,

189
00:17:07,701 --> 00:17:14,700
There's always a backup of two systems so that the aircraft is able to fly safely and continue on its journey.

190
00:17:14,701 --> 00:17:21,700
It's got onboard computers that can help do diagnosis of any technical faults that exist

191
00:17:21,701 --> 00:17:27,700
and communicate those automatically to ground stations so that if a repair is required or a part is required,

192
00:17:27,701 --> 00:17:34,700
that part can be dispatched or shipped while the aircraft is still en route to its original destination.

193
00:17:34,701 --> 00:17:40,140
That technology will enable us to keep people's schedules and trips uninterrupted,

194
00:17:40,260 --> 00:17:43,340
even though there will be some technical problems from time to time.

195
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:49,420
The Global Express also has the highest technology wing in the industry,

196
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:52,680
what it calls a supercritical wing.

197
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:57,420
It's able to be very flexible in flight.

198
00:17:57,421 --> 00:18:01,700
So it has a movable leading edge and a trailing edge,

199
00:18:01,701 --> 00:18:06,000
which allows it to be very flexible on takeoff,

200
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:08,460
and take off at very low speeds.

201
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:14,120
This corporate jet will take off at slightly more than 100 miles per hour,

202
00:18:14,740 --> 00:18:17,580
even though it cruises at nearly 600 miles an hour.

203
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:21,820
As I like to say, the Global Express can float like a butterfly

204
00:18:21,821 --> 00:18:25,980
because it's very docile, but can fly at very high speeds

205
00:18:25,981 --> 00:18:28,360
because after you take off and climb,

206
00:18:28,361 --> 00:18:32,560
It's able to reshape the wing to a very efficient cruising structure.

207
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:36,080
It gets into very small fields very comfortably.

208
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:38,340
It needs very little room to land.

209
00:18:43,320 --> 00:18:47,000
The fourth corporate jet in this category is the Gulfstream V.

210
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:52,420
Inside the cabin, it's just over 50 feet long and 7.5 feet wide.

211
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:58,100
Plenty of room for the Gulfstream design team to create its plush interior.

212
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:00,360
to the client specifications.

213
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:08,040
Upholstered walls and ceilings, rare wood and marble veneers,

214
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:12,300
luxurious leather seating and anything from calfskin to iguana.

215
00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:15,980
Even the commode in the lavatory is covered in leather.

216
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:22,620
When it comes to any of these massive jets, the buyer can get just about anything he wants.

217
00:19:23,740 --> 00:19:25,960
A discotheque was installed in one.

218
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:31,080
They have been flying party rooms,

219
00:19:31,460 --> 00:19:36,960
but also they have been the most strategic military communication centers you can imagine.

220
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:44,640
So the whole gambit of serious to frivolous have been involved in Gulfstream interiors over the years.

221
00:19:45,720 --> 00:19:49,840
These massive corporate jets are the ideal rich person's charter.

222
00:19:50,460 --> 00:19:53,060
With the boom in private jet ownership comes another trend.

223
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:55,380
Well-appointed private terminals.

224
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:57,220
