00:32:54,821 --> 00:33:04,820
Next, NASA's revolutionary new airline system with jet-powered air taxis and smart airports.

377
00:33:04,821 --> 00:33:11,820
And, visionary aircraft developer Burt Rutan on the future of private jets.

378
00:33:11,821 --> 00:33:17,820
In 1903, as Orville and Wilbur Wright struggled to get their canvas and wooden airplane to take flight,

379
00:33:17,821 --> 00:33:23,820
it is unlikely that either of them could have imagined the future they were setting in motion.

380
00:33:23,821 --> 00:33:30,580
Pilotless jets programmed to avoid collisions in the air, as well as to take off and land

381
00:33:30,581 --> 00:33:35,420
themselves at smart airports.

382
00:33:35,421 --> 00:33:41,040
And hypersonic travel made possible by air-breathing scramjet technology that plucks the oxygen

383
00:33:41,041 --> 00:33:45,060
it needs for combustion right out of the atmosphere.

384
00:33:45,061 --> 00:33:48,420
A far cry from today's air transportation system.

385
00:33:48,421 --> 00:33:51,740
We've had airliners for roughly 65 years.

386
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:54,360
Airline travel.

387
00:33:55,420 --> 00:34:02,760
I believe 65 years from now, we'll look back and say that was kind of a phase that we went through.

388
00:34:03,160 --> 00:34:04,760
It worked for the time being.

389
00:34:05,100 --> 00:34:07,000
It got initially clumsy.

390
00:34:07,780 --> 00:34:11,800
And then it got overtaken by other technologies.

391
00:34:11,801 --> 00:34:20,620
Gulfstream has not given up on its dream for a supersonic business jet.

392
00:34:21,340 --> 00:34:26,860
They have preliminary designs in the works and are working with major engine manufacturers

393
00:34:26,861 --> 00:34:31,420
to find a combination that may be suitable for a corporate business jet.

394
00:34:31,421 --> 00:34:40,820
Many people think that we should skip the 1.2 to 1.6 Mach speeds and go right to a hypersonic business jet.

395
00:34:41,800 --> 00:34:46,920
A hypersonic aircraft would travel at a maximum speed of Mach 10.

396
00:34:47,740 --> 00:34:55,460
That's ten times the speed that sound waves travel, or an incredible 7,420 miles per hour.

397
00:34:57,060 --> 00:35:03,960
To achieve this, NASA is developing technology for air-breathing engines called scramjets

398
00:35:03,961 --> 00:35:08,860
