443
00:38:59,640 --> 00:39:02,260
I lost 96, sold for the Delta 10.

444
00:39:02,261 --> 00:39:10,040
NASA figures that SATs will reduce transportation times to more communities by half in 10 years,

445
00:39:10,600 --> 00:39:12,940
and by two-thirds in 25 years.

446
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:19,100
But the immediate future of private jets may look like this prototype.

447
00:39:19,860 --> 00:39:23,160
Built and tested by visionary aircraft developer Bert Rutan,

448
00:39:23,700 --> 00:39:28,380
for Visionaire of St. Louis, Missouri, the aircraft is called the Vantage.

449
00:39:28,381 --> 00:39:37,020
We set out to build an aircraft that was both fast, affordable, comfortable, but also one

450
00:39:37,021 --> 00:39:39,140
that was environmental friendly.

451
00:39:39,141 --> 00:39:45,260
We wanted to make sure that from a sound standpoint that we could go in and out of small cities,

452
00:39:45,261 --> 00:39:48,240
large cities, and not interrupt the people.

453
00:39:48,241 --> 00:39:57,720
In order to accomplish this, Visionaire chose a Pratt Whitney JT15D5 turbofan, or fanjet

454
00:39:57,721 --> 00:40:01,329
engine, known for being extremely quiet.

455
00:40:01,330 --> 00:40:05,840
They also decided on a single jet engine with dual

456
00:40:05,841 --> 00:40:08,799
intakes rather than twin jets.

457
00:40:08,800 --> 00:40:14,420
Jet engines are so reliable that a single engine business

458
00:40:14,421 --> 00:40:21,600
jet would still be many times safer than even a twin engine airplane that has propellers.

459
00:40:21,601 --> 00:40:32,680
For one thing, if you have a single-engine airplane, the FAA requires you to design it so that its stall speed or minimum flying speed is relatively low.

460
00:40:33,680 --> 00:40:42,977
And what that means is if you become a glider and you land out in a field somewhere, your chances of survival are very good compared to very poor if you land real fast

461
00:40:42,978 --> 00:40:44,480
like a common business jet.

462
00:40:44,481 --> 00:40:53,920
While several aircraft companies build their jets fuselage entirely out of composite materials,

463
00:40:54,560 --> 00:40:57,800
the Vantage will be the first all-composite business jet.

464
00:40:59,380 --> 00:41:04,400
The composite construction techniques use computer-controlled automated machines

465
00:41:04,401 --> 00:41:08,800
to literally weave the structure from individual carbon fiber threads

466
00:41:08,801 --> 00:41:12,220
over a composite epoxy honeycomb core.

467
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:20,220
The Vantage's airframe is composed of this material that is then crafted into a seamless fuselage.

468
00:41:21,580 --> 00:41:27,220
You can mold this in very large parts, and therefore you don't need all the rivets that you use in other airplane

469
00:41:27,221 --> 00:41:29,260
where you have to use a lot of smaller parts.

470
00:41:29,980 --> 00:41:35,960
And with composites, you can get beautiful compound curves, so it makes them far more aerodynamic airplanes.

471
00:41:36,480 --> 00:41:41,120
It's really the wave of the future as far as we're concerned in general aviation aircraft.

472
00:41:41,121 --> 00:41:51,240
With six passengers and one pilot, it will have a range of 1,000 miles at a maximum cruise speed of over 400 miles per hour.

473
00:41:51,900 --> 00:41:56,400
It will fly up to 41,000 feet, which takes it higher than most weather.

474
00:41:57,720 --> 00:42:01,680
And it will land on a runway that's only 2,500 feet long.

475
00:42:01,681 --> 00:42:07,460
We feel that there's a tremendous future going forward for private business jets,

476
00:42:07,560 --> 00:42:13,220
but also there are going to be personal jets that sell for about half of what this jet does.

477
00:42:13,840 --> 00:42:18,260
And as we look to the future, we're going to see many, many thousands of jets built.

478
00:42:18,261 --> 00:42:23,300
And in fact, we'll start seeing people trained in jets rather than in propeller aircraft.

479
00:42:25,100 --> 00:42:29,460
But with smaller jets, more airports, and an increase in air traffic,

480
00:42:29,461 --> 00:42:33,540
There is always the question, won't the skies become too crowded?

481
00:42:34,260 --> 00:42:36,400
Some people think they're too crowded already.

482
00:42:39,140 --> 00:42:43,720
Anyone that says the skies are crowded, ask them to run outside and look up.

483
00:42:45,400 --> 00:42:46,100
Anywhere.

484
00:42:47,160 --> 00:42:50,920
And in the most congested areas, on a very clear day,

485
00:42:51,060 --> 00:42:55,040
when you can see 20,000 cubic miles of air,

486
00:42:55,420 --> 00:42:57,380
you might see two airplanes.

487
00:42:57,880 --> 00:42:58,880
At the most, three.

488
00:42:59,460 --> 00:43:03,460
But what about a more distant future?

489
00:43:03,461 --> 00:43:09,460
Burt Rutan envisions small future airports that operate something like aircraft carriers.

490
00:43:09,461 --> 00:43:15,460
Currently, an aircraft carrier wastes the energy from trapping an aircraft as it lands.

491
00:43:15,461 --> 00:43:18,460
Rutan believes this energy can be used.

492
00:43:18,461 --> 00:43:26,460
Essentially, you can put 20 airports right downtown just by stacking them.

493
00:43:26,461 --> 00:43:30,740
and an airplane comes in and is decelerated in two or three hundred feet.

494
00:43:31,620 --> 00:43:36,040
And then the energy of decelerating that airplane is used to accelerate the next airplane.

495
00:43:37,320 --> 00:43:39,780
And that can be done much quieter, much more convenient.

496
00:43:41,160 --> 00:43:47,620
In a single century, we progressed from horses and buckboards to two cars in every garage.

497
00:43:47,621 --> 00:43:54,900
Whatever the long-range future of private jets, it is clear that the way we use large

498
00:43:54,901 --> 00:43:59,340
airliners and airport hubs today is in a state of transformation.

499
00:44:03,620 --> 00:44:08,640
Private jet ownership may write the next chapter in our transportation history.

500
00:44:10,400 --> 00:44:14,080
In a sense, we put wheels on America in the last century.

501
00:44:14,081 --> 00:44:21,860
I don't think it's too far of a stretch to picture that in the 21st century, in a like way, we could put wings on America.

502
00:44:27,620 --> 00:44:28,740
Next on History.

503
00:44:29,240 --> 00:44:32,920
It's amazing what technologies folks came up with to get across the prairie.

504
00:44:33,140 --> 00:44:34,660
Take the wind wagon, for example.

505
00:44:34,940 --> 00:44:37,240
Basically an 8-foot-long boat with wheels.

506
00:44:37,580 --> 00:44:39,560
When the wind was whipping, you were literally sailing.

507
00:44:39,860 --> 00:44:41,960
When it wasn't, all you could do was push.

508
00:44:41,961 --> 00:44:46,220
Navigating the road west on Wild West Tech, next on the History Channel.

